#X text 82 2542 Timbre \, octave and the modulations of the tone generators. Beyond har and oct nothing here is on the Poly-800. All edges stay band-limited. In this order: timbre \, octave \, PWM \, Walsh loop \, sync \, ring. \; har1 \, har2 (45): timbre of DCO1 / DCO2 \, the same as the controls above (see Timbre). \; hmg (0): the MG moves har1 and har2 by +-hmg steps \, 127 = through the whole timbre path (rate mgr / mgf \, delay mgd). \; oct1 \, oct2 (0): -1 0 1 = LOW MID HIGH \, 2-4: the feet one to three octaves higher (the combinations without 16'). \; pw1 \, pw2 (64): pulse width 0-127 = 3-97 % \, 64 = square. Every foot becomes the AND of itself and a copy shifted by part of its period (pulse staircases on the saw net). \; pwm1 \, pwm2 (0): the MG moves the width \, 127 = +-47 %. \; Walsh loop: the four feet are square waves of one counter \, an octave apart. Multiplying them (XOR of the counter bits) gives 15 Walsh functions \, square-like waves with 1 to 8 cycles per 16' period (sequency) and their energy near that harmonic. They come from the same counter edges \, so they are exactly in tune and band-limited - a wavetable the chip already has. The loop orders them by sequency: moving along it sweeps the energy from the 16' up to the 8th harmonic and back \, neighbours fading at equal power. \; wsh1 \, wsh2 (0): position on the loop 0-127 (0: 16' / 17: 16'x4' / 59: 2' / 85: all four / 119: 16'x8' / 127 = 0 again). \; wmx1 \, wmx2 (0): mix from the normal feet (0) to the loop (127). \; wsm (0): the MG moves the position +-wsm steps (127: the whole loop \, a sweeping \, vocal sound - try mgr 2-8). \; wse (0): DEG3 moves the position by up to wse steps (-127..127) \, e.g. a bright attack falling into the 16'. wsm and wse act where wmx is above 0. \; sync (0): 1: DCO2's counter restarts at every 16' period of DCO1 \, at the exact sub-sample time. \; swp2 (0): DCO2 offset 0-72 semitones. \; swe (0): DEG3 adds up to 0-72 semitones to DCO2. \; swm (0): the MG adds +-0-24 semitones to DCO2. Screaming sync: sync 1 \, lev1 low \, lev2 high \, swe 40-72 \, a falling DEG3 (d3 \, su3 0) \, res and drv up. The sweeps also work without sync. \; ring (0): 0-127 \, DCO2 is multiplied by a +-1 wave of DCO1 (XOR \, the ring modulation of square waves). At 127 only sum and difference tones remain \, lev1 0 for the pure ring sound. \; rmf (0): the modulator \, a step 0-14 on the Walsh loop of DCO1: 0 its 16' (softest) \, 1 8' \, 3 4' \, 7 2' \, 10 all four feet \, back down to 14 16'x8'. Steps towards 7 move the sidebands further apart: stronger \, more metallic., f 64;
